<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 8</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 8 Walkthrough: Kids Playroom</h1>

    <p>Kids Playroom is room 8 of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the first room in the chapter that is built around a single long chain rather than two independent locks. Everything in it feeds one display: six toy cars on six shelves, each with a two-digit box, and the order the numbers go in comes from a different puzzle on the other side of the room. Get the order wrong and the numbers mean nothing.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a single 4:58 recording, sampled at one frame per second and read with a text-recognition pass over every frame. The keypad code <b>30</b> was read on the wall sign, on the keypad display and again from a zoomed crop. The car values <b>05</b>, <b>66</b>, <b>50</b>, <b>06</b> and <b>08</b> each appear in at least three separate frames. The sixth value did not settle, and it is listed as unverified instead of being filled in with a guess.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={8} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Kids Playroom is room 8 of 15 in Chapter 3. Two answers: <b>30</b> on the two-digit keypad (from the sign that reads XXX over 10 + 10 + 10) and a six-part code on the toy-car display, entered in the order <b>V Z O P X H</b>: <b>05 · 66 · 50 · 06 · 08 · ?</b></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Sweep the room. Papers give you three equations: <b>P = 30 / V</b>, <b>O = Z − 16</b> and <b>X = 8</b>. A whiteboard adds <b>Z = 396 / 6</b>.</li>
        <li>Read the white sign reading <b>XXX</b> over <b>10 + 10 + 10</b>. That is <b>30</b>. Type it on the dark keypad with the yellow display — the keys are scrambled, rows of 1-4-7, 5-8-0, 2-6-9 — and the toy chest opens for a <b>Shovel</b>.</li>
        <li>Sort the letter cubes by the colour order on the wardrobe label <b>THE ORDER OF LETTER CUBE BASED ON COLOR</b>. The result is <b>V, Z, O, P, X, H</b>.</li>
        <li>Convert the letters: V = <b>05</b>, Z = 396/6 = <b>66</b>, O = 66 − 16 = <b>50</b>, P = 30/5 = <b>06</b>, X = <b>08</b>. Enter them top to bottom on the six two-digit boxes on the toy-car display.</li>
        <li>The display clears and a <b>Key</b> is revealed. The recording ends at 4:56 on a THANKS FOR WATCHING card, so no coin reward is printed here.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="s3K_9l0L5Y4" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 8 Kids Playroom Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 8 Kids Playroom Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/s3K_9l0L5Y4/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 8 Kids Playroom walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/s3K_9l0L5Y4/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 4:58 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-hero-playroom.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 8 Kids Playroom: a light blue children's table and two chairs on a round patterned rug, a yellow-cushioned storage bench with cubbies, a white cubby shelf with an orange cat toy, a wooden play castle with stairs, and a blue climbing wall with two green rings" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>A playroom, and a busy one. The floor is light wood in a herringbone pattern, the walls are white with pale blue polka dots, and the furniture is arranged around a round rug with bright flower shapes on it. A child-height table in light blue with two matching chairs sits on the rug with a colouring book and a green pencil on it. Along one wall runs a low white cubby unit with a yellow cushioned top; a blue bucket sits at its end. Facing it is a corner shelf of toys — a pink mixer, a stuffed rabbit, an owl, a teddy bear. On the third wall stands a wooden play castle with a small arched door, a window and a staircase, and beside it a blue climbing wall with coloured holds and two green gymnastic rings. A window with a potted plant, a star piñata and a white door finish the room.</p>
    <p>The structure is a chain, not a pair of locks. One keypad opens a toy chest early and hands you a Shovel. Then the room asks you to sort six letter cubes by colour, and only after that does the six-shelf car display make any sense, because its numbers are entered in the cube order rather than left to right by car colour. The numbers themselves are scattered across three pieces of paper and one whiteboard, and if you have not collected them the display will simply sit there with its boxes at 00.</p>
    <p>What makes this room slower than its size suggests is the keypad. Its keys are not in telephone order — the rows read 1-4-7, 5-8-0, 2-6-9 — so anyone tapping by muscle memory will type a different number than they intend and get no feedback beyond a locked chest.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-car-track-puzzle.jpg" alt="A puzzle panel in Kids Playroom titled MOVE THE CAR ACCORDING TO THE COLOR OF THE TRACK, showing two interlocking oval tracks with small car tokens on the nodes" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>Every interactive object we could place, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Car-track panel</td><td>On a low table/shelf, opened early</td><td>Titled <b>MOVE THE CAR ACCORDING TO THE COLOR OF THE TRACK</b>; drag each car onto its matching track. No code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White sign</td><td>Wall above the storage unit</td><td>Reads <b>XXX</b> over <b>10 + 10 + 10</b>. The keypad clue: <b>30</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Two-digit keypad</td><td>Dark panel, green border</td><td>Scrambled keys. Code <b>30</b>. Opens the toy chest below it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toy chest</td><td>Under the keypad</td><td>Holds toys and the <b>Shovel</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wardrobe with clothes</td><td>Corner unit</td><td>Label reads <b>THE ORDER OF LETTER CUBE BASED ON COLOR</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letter cubes</td><td>Floor puzzle in front of the castle</td><td>Six cubes to be sorted into the slots. Solved order <b>V Z O P X H</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Whiteboard</td><td>Wall near the castle</td><td>Equations including <b>Z = 396 / 6</b> and <b>K + H = 87</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toy-car display</td><td>Behind a sliding panel by the door</td><td>Six cars, six two-digit boxes. The room's main lock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Piece of paper (1)</td><td>Carried item</td><td><b>P = 30 / V</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Piece of paper (2)</td><td>Carried item</td><td><b>O = Z − 16</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Piece of paper (3)</td><td>Carried item</td><td><b>X = 8</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Piece of paper (4)</td><td>Carried item</td><td><b>V = 5</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calendar sheet</td><td>Carried item</td><td>Weekday columns lettered <b>W H F</b>; the clue for the last value, which we could not read off the display.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bottle</td><td>Carried item</td><td>Labelled H2O; collected early, no use we could see.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Baseball bat</td><td>Carried item</td><td>Tool; the action it performs is not shown clearly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Key</td><td>Revealed in the car display</td><td>The room's payout item, at 4:49.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:06</span>
      <strong>Sweep the playroom before you touch anything</strong>
      The level opens on its own title card — <b>Level 8, Kids Playroom</b> — then cuts to the room. Read it left to right: the low cubby bench with the yellow cushion, the table and chairs on the rug, the corner shelf of toys, the play castle with its staircase, and the blue climbing wall with the rings. The sparkles are dense in this room and several of them sit on scenery, so a clean pass first stops you re-opening the same cubby twice.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-hero-playroom.jpg" alt="The Kids Playroom at the start of the level, with the table, rug, cubby bench, play castle and climbing wall visible" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=6s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:06</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:11 and 0:24</span>
      <strong>Two pickups that do nothing yet</strong>
      The first collectible is a <b>Piece of paper</b>, and a plastic <b>Bottle</b> with H2O down its side follows thirteen seconds later. Neither is useful on its own. The paper later turns out to read <b>P = 30 / V</b>, and P cannot be solved until V is known, so leave it in the bag. The bottle never gets used in anything we could see; do not go looking for a second one to combine it with.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=11s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:11</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=24s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:30 · the car-track panel</span>
      <strong>Drag each car onto the track that matches its colour</strong>
      The first real puzzle is a panel titled <b>MOVE THE CAR ACCORDING TO THE COLOR OF THE TRACK</b>. Two interlocking ovals — one pink, one yellow — carry small car tokens on their nodes. The rule is in the title: a car sits on the track of its own colour. The recording spends about eight seconds on it and the panel closes with a sparkle burst. There is no number on it, so nothing here goes into the code.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-car-track-puzzle.jpg" alt="The MOVE THE CAR ACCORDING TO THE COLOR OF THE TRACK panel in Kids Playroom, with pink and yellow interlocking tracks and car tokens" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=30s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:41 · the first equation</span>
      <strong>P = 30 / V</strong>
      Opened from the bag, the paper shows one line in marker: <b>P = 30 / V</b>. This is the equation that makes the chain work, because it links a letter to the keypad code. Once V = 5, P = 30 / 5 = <b>6</b>, which the display wants written as two digits: <b>06</b>. The paper is shown at 1:41 and reappears in the inventory at 2:33 and 3:09.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-paper-p-30-v.jpg" alt="A Piece of paper in Kids Playroom showing the equation P = 30 / V" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=101s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:41</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:57 · the second paper</span>
      <strong>O = Z − 16</strong>
      The next paper reads <b>O = Z − 16</b>, the same shape of clue: one letter defined by another. Z arrives on the whiteboard later as <b>Z = 396 / 6</b>, which is <b>66</b>, so O = 66 − 16 = <b>50</b>. Two of the six values are now fixed and neither needed a guess.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-paper-o-z-16.jpg" alt="A Piece of paper in Kids Playroom showing the equation O = Z - 16" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=117s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:57</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 2:05 · the sign</span>
      <strong>XXX over 10 + 10 + 10</strong>
      A white sign on the wall carries two lines: <b>XXX</b> above, <b>10 + 10 + 10</b> below. Three tens are thirty, and the keypad beside it has a two-digit display. The answer is <b>30</b>. This is the one code in the room you can solve without opening anything — the clue is printed a metre from the lock.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=125s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:05</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 2:12 · the keypad</span>
      <strong>Type 30 — and mind the key layout</strong>
      The keypad has a dark body, a green border and a yellow display strip above twelve keys. Look at the layout before typing: the rows are <b>1 4 7</b>, <b>5 8 0</b> and <b>2 6 9</b>, with a clear key and an enter arrow on the bottom row. It is not a telephone pad. Type <b>3</b> then <b>0</b>, the display reads <b>30</b>, and the toy chest below opens on the next beat for a <b>Shovel</b> — a cheese wedge, a red toy pig and a football share the box with it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-keypad-code-30.jpg" alt="The two-digit keypad in Kids Playroom with 30 in its yellow display, above a scrambled key layout" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=132s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 2:33 to 3:09 · the wardrobe</span>
      <strong>THE ORDER OF LETTER CUBE BASED ON COLOR</strong>
      A <b>Baseball bat</b> comes into the bag at 2:33, then the puzzle that actually matters: a wardrobe stands open with folded clothes in a colour order, and its label reads <b>THE ORDER OF LETTER CUBE BASED ON COLOR</b>. Six cubes on the floor are to be slotted in that colour order. The recording works the slots for about a minute, shuffling cubes in and out, before the top row finally settles.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=189s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:09</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 3:25 · the solved order</span>
      <strong>V Z O P X H</strong>
      The answer to the cube puzzle, and the index for everything after it. Write it down in this order. The car display does not care which car is which colour; it cares that the first box belongs to V, the second to Z, the third to O, the fourth to P, the fifth to X and the sixth to H.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-letter-cubes-order.jpg" alt="The letter-cube puzzle in Kids Playroom solved with the cubes reading V Z O P X H across the top row" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=205s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:25</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 3:30 · the car display</span>
      <strong>Six shelves, six boxes, all at 00</strong>
      Behind a sliding panel by the door is the room's main lock: six toy cars on six shelves, with a column of six orange two-digit boxes to their right. The panel header states the rule: <b>FILL IN THE NUMBERS ON THE CAR DISPLAY BASED ON THE ORDER OF THE LETTERS FROM THE LETTER CUBES THAT WERE ARRANGED EARLIER.</b> Every box sits at <b>00</b> until you start, and tapping one opens a keypad, so you fill a pair at a time.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=210s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 3:53 to 4:34 · the last clues</span>
      <strong>Z = 396 / 6, V = 5, X = 8</strong>
      A whiteboard near the castle carries several handwritten lines; the one that matters is <b>Z = 396 / 6</b>, because 396 ÷ 6 = <b>66</b> and that single value unlocks both Z and O. Two more papers close the arithmetic afterwards: <b>V = 5</b> (which also settles P, since 30 / 5 = 6) and <b>X = 8</b>. Every letter now has a value except H, whose clue is a calendar sheet with weekday columns lettered <b>W H F</b> shown at 4:42.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-whiteboard-equations.jpg" alt="The whiteboard in Kids Playroom with handwritten equations including Z = 396 / 6 and K + H = 87" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=233s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:53</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=274s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:34</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 4:47 and 4:49 · the payout</span>
      <strong>05 · 66 · 50 · 06 · 08, and the Key</strong>
      Filled top to bottom the boxes read <b>05</b>, <b>66</b>, <b>50</b>, <b>06</b>, <b>08</b>, then a sixth pair that never sat still in our frames. That is the chain in one column: V = 5 padded to 05, Z = 396/6 = 66, O = 66 − 16 = 50, P = 30/5 = 6 padded to 06, X = 8 padded to 08. The pad is positional, not numeric. When the display is accepted the orange boxes vanish and a large <b>Key</b> is revealed where they were; it goes into the bag, and the recording ends at 4:56 on a THANKS FOR WATCHING card.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-car-display-code.jpg" alt="The toy-car display in Kids Playroom with the two-digit boxes filled 05, 66, 50, 06 and 08 beside the six cars" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-key-reward.jpg" alt="A large key revealed in the toy-car display panel in Kids Playroom after the code is accepted" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l8-end-card.jpg" alt="The THANKS FOR WATCHING end card that closes the Kids Playroom recording" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=287s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:47</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=289s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:49</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s3K_9l0L5Y4&t=296s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:56</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p><b>The keypad will not take 30.</b> Check the layout, not the number. The keys are scrambled into rows of <b>1 4 7</b>, <b>5 8 0</b> and <b>2 6 9</b>, so tapping the second cell of the bottom row does not give you 0 the way a phone pad would. Look at the display after each press; the digits appear in the yellow strip above the keys.</p>
    <p><b>The car display takes the numbers and nothing happens.</b> The order is the puzzle. Boxes are read V, Z, O, P, X, H top to bottom, not red-to-silver by car colour. If you entered the values in colour order, or sorted them ascending, the panel will not release even though every individual number is right.</p>
    <p><b>You cannot find H's value.</b> Neither could we, cleanly. The clue is the calendar sheet with the weekday columns lettered <b>W H F</b>, and the whiteboard's <b>K + H = 87</b> is only useful if you have K. This page does not print a sixth number because two readings of that box disagreed between frames, and a wrong digit costs you the whole code.</p>
    <p><b>P = 30 / V looks like it needs the keypad code.</b> It does, and that is the point — the room reuses 30. Once V = 5 the sum is 6, and the display wants <b>06</b>. Writing 6 alone will not fit the box.</p>
    <p><b>The car-track puzzle seems unsolvable.</b> It is a colour-matching drag, not a sequence. Every car belongs on the track of its own colour; the interlocking ovals are there to make you think a move order matters when it does not.</p>

    <h2>Where Kids Playroom sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Kids Playroom is room <b>8 of 15</b>. It follows Master Bathroom and precedes Backyard, which is the first room of the chapter that moves outdoors. The chapter's own title card in this recording reads <b>Level 8 — Kids Playroom</b>.</p>
    <p>It is also the chapter's first real chain room. Earlier rooms in Chapter 3 tend to give you two independent locks that can be solved in either order; this one cannot be finished until the cube sort is done, because the cube order is the index for the car display. If you are working through the chapter in order, expect the rooms from here to lean harder on one puzzle feeding another.</p>

